# Executing temporal specifications: run sessions, stop, memory, and I/O.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.
## The G (F ...) specification near the end needs ltlsynt (from Spot) on
## the PATH.

# The run command of demo_3.x executes safety (always-style) specifications.
# On this branch it also takes a step count, keeps a paused session between
# calls, and executes genuine LTL specifications by synthesizing a strategy
# first.

# Streams are declared as before. The defs listing is only reliable before a
# run, so look at it now:

o1:tau := out console.
i1:tau := in file("../tests/integration/test_files/tau-alternating_zeros_and_ones-length_10.in").
defs

# run N steps <spec> executes exactly N steps and returns to the prompt --
# no continue? gate -- and the session is kept alive:

run 3 steps G (o1[t] = 1)

# memory shows the variable bindings the paused session is carrying:

memory

# run N steps, with no formula, continues the same session where it left
# off, and stop discards it:

run 2 steps
stop

# An input stream from a file: this run mirrors the file, which holds one
# tau value per line, alternating F. and T. -- note the trailing period, a
# tau value is a whole specification:

run 8 steps G (o1[t]:tau = i1[t]:tau)
stop

# A file stream is read once and never rewound, so reading the same file
# again needs a fresh stream. This one is consumed with a delay of one
# step, so step 0 is a warm-up in which only the input moves:

i2:tau := in file("../tests/integration/test_files/tau-alternating_zeros_and_ones-length_10.in").
run 7 steps G (o1[t]:tau = i2[t-1]:tau)
stop

# Output can go to a file as well. Check the result afterwards with
# cat /tmp/tau_demo_5.3.out :

i3:tau := in file("../tests/integration/test_files/tau-alternating_zeros_and_ones-length_10.in").
o2:tau := out file("/tmp/tau_demo_5.3.out").
run 8 steps G (o2[t]:tau = i3[t]:tau)
stop

# A top-level F is the same operator as sometimes, so it executes through
# the classical machinery like any safety-fragment specification. A
# genuinely reactive specification -- here a nested liveness, grant
# infinitely often -- is executed by first calling ltlsynt for a strategy
# and then stepping that strategy:

run 2 steps G (F (o1[t] = 1))
stop

# Console input works as in demo_3.1: the interpreter prompts per time
# point, and a tau value ends with a period. The run below is constrained
# only at time 0, so it stops needing input after one value; at the
# continue? gate, q ends the run:

i4:tau := in console.
run o1[0] = i4[0]
T.
q

quit
